    {
      "id": "question",
      "number": 2,
      "title": "The Record",
      "summary": "Agent Fox examines declassified paperwork while a vintage map and archive labels drift behind him.",
      "duration": 27,
      "authoredDuration": 14,
      "transition": "ink",
      "narration": "Let's start with what the CIA itself told the Senate. According to the CIA director's testimony, MKUltra ran from 1953 to 1964. That's eleven years. The surviving records list 149 subprojects. They name 80 institutions, and 44 of them were colleges and universities. They also name 185 researchers and assistants from outside the government.",
      "keywords": [
        "1953",
        "1964",
        "eleven years",
        "149 subprojects",
        "80 institutions",
        "44",
        "185"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "experiments",
      "number": 3,
      "title": "The Experiments",
      "summary": "A laboratory sketch: a glass vial, paperwork and a reel-to-reel recorder animate into view.",
      "duration": 27,
      "authoredDuration": 14,
      "transition": "flash",
      "narration": "So what were all these subprojects actually doing? The director sorted them into categories. Among them: drugs and alcohol. Hypnosis. Sleep research. Polygraphs. The record also covers drugs meant as aids to interrogation. LSD was among the drugs tested. And here is the part that matters most. Some of the people tested were volunteers. Some were never told.",
      "keywords": [
        "categories",
        "LSD",
        "interrogation",
        "volunteers",
        "never told"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cost",
      "number": 4,
      "title": "The Human Cost",
      "summary": "Evidence of testing without informed consent. The room turns quiet and serious.",
      "duration": 16,
      "authoredDuration": 18,
      "transition": "fade",
      "narration": "The testimony counted six subprojects that tested drugs on unwitting people. Some of those tests happened in safe houses in San Francisco and New York City. There was no informed consent.",
      "keywords": [
        "six subprojects",
        "unwitting",
        "safe houses",
        "no informed consent"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ],
      "beatsByLang": {
        "en": [
          {
            "cue": "six subprojects",
            "kind": "waffle",
            "total": 149,
            "hit": 6,
            "label": "6 OF 149 SUBPROJECTS",
            "note": "tested drugs on people who did not know"
          },
          {
            "cue": "unwitting people",
            "kind": "define",
            "word": "un·wit·ting",
            "pos": "adjective",
            "meaning": "not knowing; unaware of what is happening"
          },
          {
            "cue": "no informed consent",
            "kind": "define",
            "word": "in·formed con·sent",
            "pos": "noun",
            "meaning": "agreeing to an experiment after being told what it involves"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-5asb3j",
      "number": 5,
      "title": "The Olson Case",
      "summary": "A painted cutaway away from the office: an empty 1950s meeting room while the Frank Olson case is told.",
      "duration": 15,
      "authoredDuration": 15,
      "transition": "fade",
      "narration": "One case shows what that meant. Dr. Frank Olson was an Army civilian employee. In 1953, at a meeting called by the CIA, he was given LSD without knowing it. About a week later, he was dead.",
